WEBVTT

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.453
Does that better fit improve forecasts?

00:00:02.903 --> 00:00:09.470
We compared all three distributions in 27 groups: each dataset, each pipeline, each period.

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:15.982
By lowest mean squared error, GED wins 10, Student t 13, and Gaussian 4.

00:00:16.432 --> 00:00:22.359
By mean absolute error, GED wins 18, Student t 6, and Gaussian 3.

00:00:22.809 --> 00:00:24.865
But counting wins hides the size.

00:00:25.315 --> 00:00:27.064
Look at the actual changes.

00:00:27.514 --> 00:00:32.950
For Tunisia's DWT model, Student t cuts MSE by 6.6 percent.

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:39.354
For Infosys' DWT model in the later years, it raises MSE by 1.4 percent.

00:00:39.804 --> 00:00:45.371
For Google's MODWT model in the later years, it cuts MSE by 1.2 percent.

00:00:45.821 --> 00:00:50.445
For South Africa's raw GARCH, it cuts MSE by 0.1 percent.

00:00:50.895 --> 00:00:57.410
And for IBM's DWT model in the later years, Student t cuts MSE by 1.3 percent.

00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:03.841
Heavier tails help more often than not, but the gain depends on the data, and it can be negative.
